// E3's fork-join fold, end to end on one thread: par_reduce chains one
// spawned child per element — the callable's own function entered with
// the running accumulator and the element, each joined before the next
// spawns — so the reduction applies strictly in input order (the left
// fold's data dependency, deterministic replay by construction). The
// combinator is a @std library fn over the fork-join engine seam, so
// the free generic body's one-word instantiation boundary binds the
// fold's init to one-word element types through this spelling (the
// multi-word fold rides the engine seam until the generic-body staging
// round widens it); the single-thread slice's spawn-and-join overhead
// against the sequential reduce is the baseline the later scheduler
// round measures from.

fn main() -> () {
  const xs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  const sum = xs.par_reduce(0, (a: int, b: int) => a + b)
  println("sum ${sum}")
  const words = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]
  const joined = words.par_reduce("", (a: string, b: string) => a + b)
  println("joined ${joined}")
  const empty = xs.par_reduce(100, (a: int, b: int) => a * b)
  println("empty ${empty}")
}
